# Why hasn't OpenAI simply shipped "Start a company"?

I think there are three structural reasons.

First, **they don't need to own every vertical**. Their explicit strategy with apps is to let third-party developers bring capabilities into ChatGPT. ([OpenAI Help Center][8])

Second, “start a company” is not actually one tool. It's dozens of country-, platform- and business-specific systems:

```text
identity
banking
tax
corporate registration
commerce
email
phone
inventory
accounting
marketplaces
advertising
shipping
payments
licensing
```

OpenAI is much better positioned as the orchestration layer while Shopify, Intuit, HubSpot, etc. expose tools underneath. And tellingly, OpenAI is actively promoting partner plugins from companies including **QuickBooks, HubSpot and Gusto** to Business users. ([OpenAI Help Center][9])

Third, the hard part isn't **creating** a store.

AI has nearly commoditized that already.

The hard thing is:

> **What should this business do next to make money?**

That's BreadUp.

---

## So the urgency is real, but slightly different

I would assume that within a fairly short period ChatGPT will make this trivial:

> “Create me a Shopify store selling vintage records.”

Don't build that as the moat.

Assume OpenAI/Shopify win it.

Build:

> **“Find me a business worth creating.”**

Then:

> **“Continuously find profitable actions for that business.”**

And finally:

> **“Execute those actions within my risk/budget policy.”**

That's harder and much more defensible.

The compressed thesis is now:

> **Shopify provisions the store. Cmail provisions the identity. ChatGPT operates the interface. BreadUp decides where the money is.**

That is much sharper than “AI entrepreneur app.”

